---
import DocsLayout from '../../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import DocFigure from '../../../../components/DocFigure.astro';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Quick Start — MHA Manager"
  description="Run your first MHA Manager job in five steps."
  currentSlug="quick-start"
>

  <div class="doc-header">
    <h1 class="doc-title">Quick Start</h1>
    <p class="doc-lead">
      The fastest path to results — a single Mono job from open panel to exported AnimSequence.
      Assumes the plugin is already installed and the required engine plugins are enabled.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-tip">
    <strong>New to MetaHuman Animator?</strong> A Mono job needs only a
    <code>UFootageCaptureData</code> asset — the footage import for your take. No MetaHuman Identity
    or Camera Calibration is required (those are for Stereo takes). Bring footage in through Live Link
    Hub / Capture Manager and the Capture Data asset is created for you.
  </div>

  <div class="steps">

    <div class="step">
      <div class="step-num">1</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <h3 class="step-title">Open the MHA Manager panel</h3>
        <p class="step-desc">
          From the menu bar, go to <strong>Tools → MHA Manager</strong>.
          The panel docks into the editor with the queue and the <strong>Global Settings</strong> section both on screen. You can float or re-dock it anywhere.
        </p>
        <DocFigure
          src="/assets/docs/mha/qs-panel-empty.webp"
          alt="The MHA Manager tab open and docked in the Unreal editor"
          caption="The MHA Manager panel on first open — empty queue with Global Settings at the top"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step">
      <div class="step-num">2</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <h3 class="step-title">Choose where outputs land</h3>
        <p class="step-desc">
          By default, generated assets are saved next to the source Capture Data asset. To collect
          everything in one place instead, enable <strong>UE Export Directory</strong> in the
          settings panel and point it at a Content Browser folder — for example
          <code>/Game/MHA_Batch</code> (the default when enabled).
        </p>
        <DocFigure
          src="/assets/docs/mha/qs-global-settings.webp"
          alt="The Global Settings panel showing the Ingest options and Watch Folder field"
          caption="The Global Settings panel — output and ingest options live here"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step">
      <div class="step-num">3</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <h3 class="step-title">Add a job</h3>
        <p class="step-desc">
          Click <strong>Add Jobs</strong> and pick your Capture Data. A new row appears in the
          queue with its job type already set — the plugin classifies the take and applies the
          matching solve template automatically.
        </p>
        <p class="step-desc" style="margin-top:12px;">
          Any dependent data the job type needs is auto-discovered where possible — check the
          row's cells and fill in anything missing. The classification is just a default: you
          can change the job type on the row at any time. See
          <a href="/marketplace/mha-manager/docs/job-types">Job Types</a> for what each type
          requires.
        </p>
        <DocFigure
          src="/assets/docs/mha/qs-job-configured.webp"
          alt="The MHA Manager queue with the Add Jobs and Start Batch controls and Job Settings panel"
          caption="A configured job — capture data assigned, type classified automatically"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step">
      <div class="step-num">4</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <h3 class="step-title">Start the batch</h3>
        <p class="step-desc">
          Click <strong>Start Batch</strong>. The plugin will validate the job, then run it
          through the full pipeline:
        </p>
        <div class="pipeline-steps">
          <span class="pipeline-step">Validate Input</span>
          <span class="pipeline-arrow">→</span>
          <span class="pipeline-step">Process Performance</span>
          <span class="pipeline-arrow">→</span>
          <span class="pipeline-step">Export Outputs</span>
          <span class="pipeline-arrow">→</span>
          <span class="pipeline-step">Save Assets</span>
        </div>
        <p class="step-desc" style="margin-top:12px;">
          Progress is shown per-job in the queue row. The log panel below the queue streams
          live output from the solver.
        </p>
        <DocFigure
          src="/assets/docs/mha/qs-batch-running.webp"
          alt="A Mono job in the queue with status and progress columns as the batch starts"
          caption="The batch running — per-job progress bar and live log output visible"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step">
      <div class="step-num">5</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <h3 class="step-title">Find your outputs</h3>
        <p class="step-desc">
          When the job completes, your generated assets appear in the output path you set in step 2:
        </p>
        <div class="asset-table">
          <div class="asset-row header">
            <span>Asset type</span>
            <span>Description</span>
          </div>
          <div class="asset-row">
            <span><code>UMetaHumanPerformance</code></span>
            <span>The solved performance — re-processable any time</span>
          </div>
          <div class="asset-row">
            <span><code>AnimSequence</code></span>
            <span>Baked facial animation on the MetaHuman skeleton</span>
          </div>
          <div class="asset-row">
            <span><code>LevelSequence</code></span>
            <span>Sequencer-ready animation track (if enabled in Global Settings)</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <DocFigure
          src="/assets/docs/mha/qs-content-browser-output.webp"
          alt="Content Browser showing the output folder — Performance asset, AnimSequence, and LevelSequence visible"
          caption="Generated assets in the Content Browser after a successful batch"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-note" style="margin-top:32px;">
    <strong>Processing multiple takes?</strong> Add more jobs before clicking Start Batch —
    you don't need to run them one at a time. The queue processes them sequentially (or in
    parallel worker processes when <strong>Max Concurrent Jobs</strong> is raised) and saves
    a manifest automatically at <code>Saved/MHAManager/batch.mhajson</code> when done.
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-tip">
    <strong>Even faster:</strong> select one or more <code>UFootageCaptureData</code> assets in the
    Content Browser, right-click, and choose <strong>Process Selected Takes</strong>. The dialog
    detects each take's type, lets you pick a visualization asset, and runs without touching the
    queue panel at all.
  </div>

</DocsLayout>

<style>

  .doc-header { margin-bottom: 32px; }

  .doc-title {
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 2.4rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  .doc-lead {
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    max-width: 580px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .callout {
    padding: 14px 18px;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin-bottom: 28px;
    border-left: 3px solid;
  }

  .callout-tip {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 8%, transparent);
    border-color: var(--accent);
  }

  .callout-note {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, #f0a500 8%, transparent);
    border-color: #f0a500;
  }

  .callout strong { font-weight: 600; }

  .callout code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    padding: 1px 5px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    color: var(--accent);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .steps { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }

  .step {
    display: flex;
    gap: 24px;
    padding: 28px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .step:last-child { border-bottom: none; }

  .step-num {
    width: 28px;
    height: 28px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    color: var(--accent);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-top: 2px;
  }

  .step-title {
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }

  .step-desc {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .step-desc strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 500; }

  .step-desc code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    padding: 1px 5px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    color: var(--accent);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .asset-table {
    margin-top: 14px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .asset-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.6fr;
    gap: 16px;
    padding: 10px 16px;
    font-size: 0.825rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .asset-row:last-child { border-bottom: none; }

  .asset-row.header {
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }

  .asset-row strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 500; }

  .asset-row code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.775rem;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .pipeline-steps {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-top: 14px;
  }

  .pipeline-step {
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 5px 12px;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .pipeline-arrow {
    color: var(--accent);
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .step { flex-direction: column; gap: 12px; }
  }
</style>
